@page "/learn/quickgrid-onrowclick"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Clickable grid rows · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Clickable grid rows</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">People click a row to open it. How do you know which row they clicked?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 4 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> QuickGrid has a row event, <code>OnRowClick</code>. In .NET 10 QuickGrid had no
        row events at all, so a row was only clickable where you put a button or a clickable element of your own.
        This page shows what that costs, then the one parameter that replaces it.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a list of support tickets: the eight with the latest activity, from the app's real database. Each
        row has an <b>Open</b> button, the usual .NET 10 answer. A line above the grid counts every click on the grid,
        and every ticket that click opened.
    </p>
    <p>
        The hint under the grid says <i>Click a ticket</i>. Do that, the way people do: on the subject, on the
        customer, in the gap between.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A button in every row" Url="demo/row-click/tickets/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: a button in every row" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Click a subject or a customer. <b>Clicks on the grid</b> goes up, <b>Tickets opened</b> stays at 0, and
            no message appears: the hint asks for something this grid can't do. Only the <b>Open</b> button opens a
            ticket, and the pointer stays an arrow everywhere else on the row.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The grid never tells you about the row</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 10, QuickGrid renders each row as a plain <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code>, with nothing to hook a click to.
        The only place you can attach a handler is inside a cell, through a <code>TemplateColumn</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where a click can land, and what it does</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;tr&gt;                       <span class="dim">no handler</span>
├─ &lt;td&gt; Ticket              <span class="err">nothing happens</span>
├─ &lt;td&gt; Subject             <span class="err">nothing happens</span>
├─ &lt;td&gt; Customer            <span class="err">nothing happens</span>
└─ &lt;td&gt; &lt;button @@onclick&gt;  <span class="ok">✓ opens the ticket</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        So the row people aim at is mostly dead space. The button works, but it's a small target, and it's an extra
        column on every list screen.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Give the grid a row handler</h2>
    <p>Set <code>OnRowClick</code> to a method that takes the row's item.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid <span class="hl">TGridItem="TicketRow"</span> Items="tickets"
           <span class="hl">OnRowClick="OpenAsync"</span>&gt;
    ...
&lt;/QuickGrid&gt;

<span class="hl">async Task OpenAsync(TicketRow row)</span>
{
    open = await Desk.GetAsync(row.Id);
}</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        QuickGrid puts the click handler on each <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> and passes your method the clicked row's
        item, so the whole row is the target. <code>OpenAsync</code> itself is the method the button called; only
        the wiring moves.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Don't leave out TGridItem</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid Items="tickets" OnRowClick="OpenAsync"&gt;</pre>
        <div class="verdict bad">✗ CS1503: given a method group, the grid can't infer its item type. Name it with <code>TGridItem="TicketRow"</code>.</div>
    </div>
    <p>
        One trade-off to know before you delete the button: a row can't take keyboard focus, so
        <code>OnRowClick</code> is for the mouse. Fig. 2 drops the button to keep the comparison plain. On a real
        screen, keep a link or button in the row too; the full Support screen makes each subject a link.
    </p>
    <p>Here's the same list, with the button gone and <code>OnRowClick</code> set.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same list, with OnRowClick" Url="demo/row-click/tickets"
                Label=".NET 11: OnRowClick">
        <Caption>
            Click anywhere on a row. Each click opens that row's ticket below the grid, and the two counts stay
            equal. Hover a row: the pointer cursor comes with <code>OnRowClick</code>, and the open row stays
            highlighted.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two lists. Each is an Interactive Server island on an otherwise static page:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RowClick/TicketListNet10.razor" Feature="quickgrid-onrowclick">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's list. The <code>TemplateColumn</code> with its button is the only way in to <code>OpenAsync</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RowClick/TicketList.razor" Feature="quickgrid-onrowclick">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's list. The change is lines 9 and 10: <code>TGridItem</code> and <code>OnRowClick</code> on the
            grid, and the button column is gone. <code>OpenAsync</code> is highlighted because the grid now calls it,
            but the method is the same as Fig. 3's.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Put the click inside the cells</h2>
    <p>Without a row event, the handler has to live inside a cell. We built each of these on .NET 10:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to try it in .NET 10, and what clicking the row did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;TemplateColumn&gt;
  &lt;button @@onclick="..."&gt;Open&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/TemplateColumn&gt;</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ clicking the subject or customer does nothing; only the button opens it</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;TemplateColumn Title="Subject"&gt;
  &lt;div <span class="err">@@onclick="..."</span>&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/TemplateColumn&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">every column becomes a <code>TemplateColumn</code>, each with its own handler</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the cell's padding is dead: only the text area opens it</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid TGridItem="TicketRow"
  <span class="err">OnRowClick="OpenAsync"</span>&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">the .NET 11 markup, built against .NET 10's QuickGrid, which passes unknown attributes through to the <code>&lt;table&gt;</code></span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ builds with no warning, renders a dead <code>onrowclick</code> attribute, and clicks do nothing</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Two ways out went beyond the grid. One was JavaScript: tag each row with <code>RowClass</code>, listen for
        clicks on the table, find the row, and call back into .NET through a <code>DotNetObjectReference</code> you
        must remember to dispose. The other was CSS: one link per row whose <code>::after</code> is stretched over
        the whole row. That needs no script and keeps keyboard access, but it's CSS you add to every grid, and any
        other button in the row has to be lifted above the stretched link to stay clickable.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The same markup, now a real parameter</h2>
    <p>
        The third card's markup is exactly what Fig. 2 runs. The difference is that .NET 11's QuickGrid declares
        <code>OnRowClick</code> as an <code>EventCallback&lt;TGridItem&gt;</code>, so it binds to a parameter
        instead of falling through to the <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> as a dead attribute. If you see an
        <code>onrowclick</code> attribute in the rendered HTML, you're still on .NET 10.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Links or buttons inside a row?</b> Add <code>@@onclick:stopPropagation</code> to them. Otherwise a click on one opens the row too.</li>
        <li><b>It needs interactivity.</b> On a static SSR page the rows still render, but clicking them does nothing.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
